<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net472;net8.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <OutputType>Library</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>PSADT.PowerShellTestFixture</RootNamespace>
    <Product>PSAppDeployToolkit PowerShell Test Fixture Library</Product>
    <AssemblyTitle>PSAppDeployToolkit PowerShell Test Fixture Library</AssemblyTitle>
    <AssemblyDescription>This assembly hosts a PowerShell runspace so that the PowerShell-dependent types in PSAppDeployToolkit can be exercised through their real constructors.</AssemblyDescription>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PSAppDeployToolkit\PSAppDeployToolkit.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Nothing in PSAppDeployToolkit can be reached without a PowerShell engine: EnvironmentTable's only
    constructor takes a live PSCmdlet, ModuleDatabase holds a PSObject, and LogUtilities evaluates
    script blocks. A test process is not PowerShell, so unlike the assembly under test - which takes
    PowerShellStandard.Library with ExcludeAssets="runtime" and lets its host supply the engine - this
    one has to carry an engine of its own on .NET.

    On .NET Framework it does not. Windows PowerShell 5.1 is part of the operating system and its
    System.Management.Automation sits in the GAC as assembly version 3.0.0.0, which is exactly the
    version PowerShellStandard.Library's reference assembly declares. So the compile-time reference
    binds against the reference assembly and the runtime resolves the real engine, with no package
    and no binding redirect.

    On .NET it has to be Microsoft.PowerShell.SDK, and the two lighter options were both tried and
    rejected. PowerShellStandard.Library alone is reference-only and ships no implementation at all.
    The System.Management.Automation package opens a runspace but has no commands: it resolves
    Get-PSCallStack, Out-String, Write-Host, Write-Warning and Write-Verbose to whichever
    Microsoft.PowerShell.Utility the machine happens to have installed and then fails to load it
    against the package's own engine, with "Could not load type
    'System.Management.Automation.PSSnapIn'". Every one of those commands is on a path under test, so
    the full SDK is the only option that runs.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net472'">
    <PackageReference Include="PowerShellStandard.Library" PrivateAssets="all" ExcludeAssets="runtime" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net8.0'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.PowerShell.SDK" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The script module half of the fixture. Copied rather than embedded because Import-Module takes a
    path and reads the file itself, so a stream would not exercise it, and because the session state
    the tests hand to ModuleDatabase has to be a real module's session state for
    $Script:CommandTable to resolve at all.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="PSADT.PowerShellTestFixture.psm1" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
